<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sarai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarah, originally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, is the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> and mother of Isaac in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>, and a matriarch honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Genesis says she travelled with Abraham to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, gave her Egyptian servant Hagar to Abraham when still childless, and, after God renamed her, bore Isaac when Abraham was a hundred. She later asked that Hagar and Ishmael be sent away. According to Genesis she died aged 127 and Abraham bought a cave near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a> from Ephron the Hittite to bury her, the place later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cave-of-the-patriarchs">Cave of the Patriarchs</a>. Rabbinic tradition links her death to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binding-of-isaac">Binding of Isaac</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarah.jpg" alt="Sarah" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Jacques Joseph Tissot (1836 - 1902) (French) Details on Google Art Project · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Abram's_Counsel_to_Sarai_-_Google_Art_Project_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the biblical narrative Sarah is called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> until God renames her in Genesis 17, in the account of the covenant of circumcision, when Abram becomes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>. God then told Abram to leave, and Abram, aged 75, took Sarai and Lot to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>. During a famine they went south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>Fearing that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> would kill him to take his wife, Abram told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> to say she was his sister. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> took her into his palace and gave Abram presents, but God afflicted his household with plagues, and when Pharaoh learned that she was Abram's wife he ordered them to leave. After ten years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> without a child Sarai suggested that Abram have one with her Egyptian handmaiden Hagar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Three men visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> and said that Sarah would have a son within a year. She bore Isaac when Abraham was a hundred, and she said that God had made her laugh. At the feast on the day Isaac was weaned she saw the teenaged Ishmael mocking him and asked that he and Hagar be banished. In Gerar, Abraham again called Sarah his sister, and King Abimelech had her brought to him. Scholars debate the relation of the three stories in Genesis in which a patriarch calls his wife his sister, some holding that the Egyptian one is the oldest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sarah died aged 127. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> bought a cave near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a> from Ephron the Hittite, the first land the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> owned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> according to the narrative, and the place became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cave-of-the-patriarchs">Cave of the Patriarchs</a>. Rabbinic tradition in the Midrash links her death to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binding-of-isaac">Binding of Isaac</a>, with differing accounts of whether grief or joy killed her. The fifth-century Genesis Rabbah gives her much attention, presenting her as a model of excellence, and it has been suggested that the rabbis meant her as a Jewish alternative to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>.</p><p>The First Epistle of Peter praises her, and in Galatians 4 she and Hagar are an allegory of two covenants. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> she is unnamed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, and the account of her laughter at the angels' news is told in Surah Hud. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cave-of-the-patriarchs">Cave of the Patriarchs</a>, known to Muslims as the Sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>, is the second holiest site for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and is venerated by Christians and Muslims. Since the 1970s, scholars such as Thomas L. Thompson and John Van Seters have challenged the historicity of the patriarchs and matriarchs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
